{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966796,"nonce":"/O2pB8WoIZ0p9FjLYNdpeFYuwoE=","title":"render_primitives_overlay shipped every primitive without an AABB cull; canvas content +","message":"`Renderer::render_primitives_overlay` (blinc_gpu/src/renderer.rs around\nline 7599) is the dispatch path for the dynamic-batch overlay —\ncanvas content (node-editor closures), cn::popover, cn::toolbar,\nfocused-input backgrounds. Unlike `render_with_clear_simple`,\n`composite_frame`'s static-overlay filter, and the damaged-static\nrepaint, it did NOT call `cull_primitives`.\n\n**Visible symptom (resolved 2026-06-19)**: zoom jank in\nnode_editor_demo correlated with nodes crossing the viewport edge or\noverlapping the HUD. Every canvas-emitted primitive paid sort + draw\ncost even when scissored out; the cost varied with how many nodes\nthe closure happened to emit, producing frame-time variance.\n\n**Fix attempted, then REVERTED 2026-06-19**: tried calling\n`cull_primitives` on the input slice before\n`upload_sorted_primitives`. Test result: zoom jank got WORSE, not\nbetter. Likely reasons (under investigation in a follow-up\nworkflow):\n- `cull_primitives` allocates a `Vec\u003cGpuPrimitive\u003e` per call. On a\n  big graph the per-frame heap churn (across ~3 overlay dispatch\n  sites) introduced its own jitter that exceeded the GPU savings.\n- The cull catches the right primitives but the WORK that needs\n  to be skipped is upstream — the canvas closure already paid to\n  emit them, the aux_data has already been allocated, the prim\n  has already been pushed into the batch. By the time we cull at\n  GPU dispatch the costly work is done.\n- The right layer is node-editor's `render_frame` frustum cull\n  BEFORE the closure emits primitives — hoist\n  `visible_content_rect_padded` above the O(N) prepass sweeps so\n  off-frustum nodes pay nothing. That's still pending.\n\n**Why PRIM_TEXT bypass matters**: scroll-translated layout text\nsits in a containing-block content space whose AABB doesn't\ncorrespond to its on-screen rect. Skipping cull for type_info[0]==7\nkeeps that text visible when its parent's bounds appear off-screen.\nCanvas-emitted glyphs in content space go through the same bypass.\n\n**Why we don't cull in node-editor render_frame instead**: the\neditor's `visible_content_rect_padded` cull exists at editor.rs:1925\nbut runs INSIDE the per-element loop, after the O(N) preliminary\nsweeps (`nested_body_growth`, `disabled_nodes`, `subgraph_ref_nodes`,\n`effective_bounds`). Even when 90 % of nodes are off-frustum, those\nsweeps fire on every paint. Culling at the GPU primitive level is\nthe cheapest catch-net regardless of how many out-of-frustum\nprimitives the closure happens to emit.\n\n**Companion fixes still pending** (workflow output at\n`/private/tmp/.../tasks/w164i1bau.output`):\n- Hoist `visible_content_rect_padded` test ABOVE the O(N) sweeps in\n  node_editor's `render_frame` so off-frustum nodes skip the\n  bookkeeping too. Reduces CPU cost of large graphs.\n- Replace per-frame `Vec\u003cGpuPrimitive\u003e` allocation in\n  `cull_primitives` with a scratch `\u0026mut Vec` reused across the\n  four call sites. Drops heap churn at higher primitive counts.\n- Add `bezier-bulge AABB` to node-editor edge cull (currently uses\n  raw endpoint AABB → curves pop in/out as the endpoints cross\n  the pad band even though the visible bulge is still on-screen).\n\nRelated:\n- gotcha dynamic batch aux data — aux_data offsets must follow\n  the SAME primitive routing; if cull changes the count, aux_data\n  upload must match. `cull_primitives` returns a fresh Vec so\n  sort indices stay consistent.\n- gotcha web wheel invalidate drain storm — the burst-edge\n  invalidate gate addressed one factor; this cull addresses the\n  per-primitive cost layer.","files":null}]}